IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `sgia-interpretation-time`
IMPORT `sgia-p1-definitions`
IMPORT `sgia-p1-other`
IMPORT `sgia-p2-written-law`
IMPORT `sgia-p3-subsidiary`
IMPORT `sgia-p4-powers`
IMPORT `sgia-p5-penal-court`
IMPORT `sgia-p6-misc`

-- Interpretation Act 1965 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation in force from
-- 5 December 2025 -- THE SEAT LAYER.
--
-- SEAT: a person who has a time limit to meet under a written law (to apply, object, appeal, reply or pay),
-- or who needs to know when something takes effect, asking on which day their period ends and which of
-- their days count.
--
-- This layer IMPORTs the layer-1 modules and calls their rules; it restates none of them, and no layer-1
-- module was changed for it. Every question is phrased as that person asks it, takes plain inputs (yes/no,
-- numbers, dates, a choice written out in words, and a list of dates for public holidays), and answers in
-- a sentence. Where the Act does not say, the answer says so in those words: "The Interpretation Act does
-- not say." That answer is the encoding's explicit not-covered value, never a guess.
--
-- Provisions that address only an official (Part 4, most of Parts 3 and 6) have no question: what they mean
-- to a person is asked here only where it changes what the person may do, owe or rely on.
--
-- The public holidays are a borrowed fact (s 2(1) "public holiday": declared, proclaimed or observed under
-- a written law); the person lists the ones near the end of their period. The Interact form needs to accept a
-- list of dates for that input. The standard-time offset is the Act's own figure (8 hours ahead of UTC) unless
-- the President has notified another (s 51(2)), which is asked.

§ `Helpers for wording answers`

GIVEN x IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A STRING
`the answer to a yes or no question` x MEANS
    CONSIDER x
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "The Interpretation Act does not say."
    WHEN JUST b THEN IF b THEN "Yes." ELSE "No."

GIVEN `before the date` IS A STRING
      x IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A STRING
`the answer with a date` `before the date` x MEANS
    CONSIDER x
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "The Interpretation Act does not say."
    WHEN JUST d THEN CONCAT `before the date`, TOSTRING d, "."

GIVEN unit IS A STRING
GIVETH A MAYBE `Period unit`
`the period unit named` unit MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF unit EQUALS "days" THEN JUST Days
        IF unit EQUALS "weeks" THEN JUST Weeks
        IF unit EQUALS "months" THEN JUST Months
        IF unit EQUALS "years" THEN JUST Years
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

GIVEN x IS A STRING
GIVETH A `Proceedings a reference is made in relation to`
`the proceedings named` x MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF x EQUALS "Singapore International Commercial Court" THEN `proceedings in the Singapore International Commercial Court`
        IF x EQUALS "Family Division, Family Court or Youth Court and commenced before 15 October 2024" THEN `proceedings commenced before 15 October 2024 in the Family Division of the High Court, a Family Court or a Youth Court`
        OTHERWISE `any other proceedings`

§ `The words of the law (section 2)`

-- s 2(1). A "defined word" is one the Act explains for every written law: "person", "month", "public holiday" ...
@export What does a word that the Interpretation Act defines mean (s 2(1))?
GIVEN word IS A STRING @desc The word or expression as it appears in the law you are reading, for example month, year, person, public holiday, writing, will
GIVETH A STRING
`What does a word that the Interpretation Act defines mean` word MEANS
    CONSIDER `the Act's words defining` word
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "The Interpretation Act does not define this word."
    WHEN JUST explanation THEN explanation

-- s 2(5). A letter is "served" when the law says so, whether it was received or not, unless you can prove otherwise.
@export When is a document that was posted to me or by me treated as served (s 2(5))?
GIVEN `the law lets the document be served by post` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does an Act authorise or require the document to be served by post, whether it uses the word serve, give or send or another word?
      `the law shows a different intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the Act show an intention that posting should not count as service in this way?
      `it was properly addressed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the letter properly addressed?
      `the postage was prepaid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the postage paid in advance?
      `it was posted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the letter posted?
      `the day it would be delivered in the ordinary course of post` IS A DATE @desc On what day would the letter normally be delivered by post (YYYY-MM-DD)?
      `the contrary is proved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can it be proved that the letter was not delivered on that day?
GIVETH A STRING
`When is a posted document treated as served` `the law lets the document be served by post` `the law shows a different intention` `it was properly addressed` `the postage was prepaid` `it was posted` `the day it would be delivered in the ordinary course of post` `the contrary is proved` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the moment service by post is deemed effected` (`service by post is deemed effected` `the law lets the document be served by post` `the law shows a different intention` `it was properly addressed` `the postage was prepaid` `it was posted`) (Moment OF `the day it would be delivered in the ordinary course of post`, 0, 8) `the contrary is proved`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "The Interpretation Act does not say."
    WHEN JUST m THEN CONCAT "The document is treated as served on ", TOSTRING (m's day), "."

§ `Death (section 2A)`

-- s 2A(1). Either way of dying counts, for all purposes.
@export Has the person died in law (s 2A(1))?
GIVEN `their breathing and blood circulation have stopped for good` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have circulation of blood and respiration in the person's body irreversibly stopped?
      `their brain has totally and irreversibly stopped working` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have all functions of the person's brain totally and irreversibly stopped?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Has the person died in law` `their breathing and blood circulation have stopped for good` `their brain has totally and irreversibly stopped working` MEANS
    `a person has died` `their breathing and blood circulation have stopped for good` `their brain has totally and irreversibly stopped working`

§ `When a law starts to apply to me (sections 10 and 23)`

GIVEN how IS A STRING
GIVETH A `How the day of commencement is fixed`
`how the day is fixed from words` how MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF how EQUALS "named in the law or in a notification" THEN `specified by the written law or by a notification made under it`
        IF how EQUALS "the date of publication in the Gazette" THEN `the date of publication in the Gazette`
        OTHERWISE `fixed by neither`

-- s 10(1). The law starts "on the expiration of the previous day": the first moment of the named day.
@export On which day does the law start to bind me (s 10(1))?
GIVEN `how the day is fixed` IS A STRING @desc How does the law fix the day it starts? One of: named in the law or in a notification, the date of publication in the Gazette, neither
      `the day` IS A DATE @desc The day named by the law or the notification, or the date of publication in the Gazette (YYYY-MM-DD)
GIVETH A STRING
`On which day does the law start to bind me` `how the day is fixed` `the day` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the moment a written law comes into operation` (`how the day is fixed from words` `how the day is fixed`) `the day` 8
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "The Interpretation Act does not say."
    WHEN JUST m THEN CONCAT "The law starts to apply from the very start of ", TOSTRING (m's day), "."

@export At what time of day on that day does the law start (s 10(1), s 51)?
GIVEN `how the day is fixed` IS A STRING @desc How does the law fix the day it starts? One of: named in the law or in a notification, the date of publication in the Gazette, neither
      `the day` IS A DATE @desc The day named by the law or the notification, or the date of publication in the Gazette (YYYY-MM-DD)
      `the President has notified a different standard time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the President notified, in the Gazette, a standard time other than 8 hours ahead of UTC?
      `the notified hours ahead of UTC` IS A NUMBER @desc If so, how many hours ahead of UTC is the standard time the President notified? (Enter 8 if none was notified.)
GIVETH A STRING
`At what time of day on that day does the law start` `how the day is fixed` `the day` `the President has notified a different standard time` `the notified hours ahead of UTC` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the moment a written law comes into operation` (`how the day is fixed from words` `how the day is fixed`) `the day` (`standard time, in hours ahead of UTC` (IF `the President has notified a different standard time` THEN JUST `the notified hours ahead of UTC` ELSE NOTHING))
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "The Interpretation Act does not say."
    WHEN JUST m THEN CONCAT "At midnight at the start of ", TOSTRING (m's day), " by standard time, which is ", TOSTRING (m's `hours ahead of UTC`), " hours ahead of UTC."

-- s 23(1)(b). A regulation or other subsidiary legislation, unless it says otherwise, takes effect on its publication date.
@export On what day does a regulation take effect (s 23(1)(b))?
GIVEN `the date it was published in the Gazette` IS A DATE @desc The date the regulation, order or other subsidiary legislation was published in the Gazette (YYYY-MM-DD)
      `it names a day of its own` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the regulation itself name a different day for taking effect?
      `the day it names` IS A DATE @desc If so, which day (YYYY-MM-DD)? Enter the publication date if it names none.
GIVETH A STRING
`On what day does a regulation take effect` `the date it was published in the Gazette` `it names a day of its own` `the day it names` MEANS
    CONCAT "It takes effect on ", TOSTRING (`the day subsidiary legislation takes effect and comes into operation` `the date it was published in the Gazette` (IF `it names a day of its own` THEN JUST `the day it names` ELSE NOTHING)), "."

§ `If the law I relied on is repealed or expires (sections 11 to 18)`

GIVEN what IS A STRING
GIVETH A MAYBE `What a repeal might do`
`the repeal effect named` what MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF what EQUALS "revive something not in force" THEN JUST `revive anything not in force or existing when the repeal takes effect`
        IF what EQUALS "affect what was done under the law" THEN JUST `affect the previous operation of the repealed law or anything duly done or suffered under it`
        IF what EQUALS "affect a right or liability that had accrued" THEN JUST `affect a right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued or incurred under it`
        IF what EQUALS "affect a penalty already incurred" THEN JUST `affect a penalty, forfeiture or punishment incurred for an offence against it`
        IF what EQUALS "affect an investigation, proceeding or remedy" THEN JUST `affect an investigation, legal proceeding or remedy in respect of such a right, liability or penalty`
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- s 16(1). A repeal does not take away accrued rights, liabilities, penalties, or the right to go on with a proceeding.
@export Does the repeal of a law affect something I had already done or become entitled to or liable for (s 16(1))?
GIVEN `what I am worried about` IS A STRING @desc What could the repeal affect? One of: revive something not in force, affect what was done under the law, affect a right or liability that had accrued, affect a penalty already incurred, affect an investigation, proceeding or remedy
      `the repealing law repeals the other law in whole or in part` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law repeal the other law, in whole or in part?
      `the repealing law shows a different intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the repealing law show an intention that the repeal should have this effect?
GIVETH A STRING
`Does the repeal of a law affect something I had already done or become entitled to or liable for` `what I am worried about` `the repealing law repeals the other law in whole or in part` `the repealing law shows a different intention` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the repeal effect named` `what I am worried about`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "Please choose one of the listed answers."
    WHEN JUST w THEN CONCAT "Does the repeal do that? ", `the answer to a yes or no question` (`the repeal does this` w `the repealing law repeals the other law in whole or in part` `the repealing law shows a different intention`)

@export May an investigation, proceeding or remedy about an accrued right or penalty still go on after the repeal (s 16(1)(e))?
GIVEN `it is such an investigation, proceeding or remedy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it an investigation, legal proceeding or remedy about a right, privilege, obligation, liability, penalty, forfeiture or punishment under the repealed law?
      `the repealing law shows a different intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the repealing law show an intention that it should not go on?
GIVETH A STRING
`May an investigation, proceeding or remedy still go on after the repeal` `it is such an investigation, proceeding or remedy` `the repealing law shows a different intention` MEANS
    `the answer to a yes or no question` (`it may be instituted, continued or enforced, or the penalty imposed, as if the repealing law had not been passed` `it is such an investigation, proceeding or remedy` `the repealing law shows a different intention`)

@export If a law that repealed an older law is itself repealed, does the older law come back (s 12)?
GIVEN `the law that repealed the older law has itself been repealed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the law that repealed the older law itself been repealed?
      `words were added reviving the older law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the later repeal add words that revive the older law?
GIVETH A STRING
`If a law that repealed an older law is itself repealed does the older law come back` `the law that repealed the older law has itself been repealed` `words were added reviving the older law` MEANS
    `the answer to a yes or no question` (`the last repeal revives the written law or provisions previously repealed` `the law that repealed the older law has itself been repealed` `words were added reviving the older law`)

@export While a replacement provision is not yet in force, is the repealed law still in force (s 14)?
GIVEN `a law repeals an older law and puts something in its place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law repeal an older law, wholly or in part, and substitute other provision for it?
      `the replacement has come into operation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the replacement provision come into operation?
GIVETH A STRING
`While a replacement provision is not yet in force is the repealed law still in force` `a law repeals an older law and puts something in its place` `the replacement has come into operation` MEANS
    `the answer to a yes or no question` (`the repealed written law remains in force` `a law repeals an older law and puts something in its place` `the replacement has come into operation`)

@export A law I started a case under has expired. May my case go on (s 18)?
GIVEN `the law has expired` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the written law expired?
      `my case is civil or criminal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the proceeding a civil or a criminal one?
      `it was started under the law before it expired` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the proceeding started under the law before the law expired?
GIVETH A STRING
`A law I started a case under has expired may my case go on` `the law has expired` `my case is civil or criminal` `it was started under the law before it expired` MEANS
    `the answer to a yes or no question` (`the proceeding may be continued as if the written law continued in force` `the law has expired` `my case is civil or criminal` `it was started under the law before it expired`)

§ `If I am charged with an offence (sections 38 to 41)`

@export Can I be charged with an attempt to commit this offence (s 38)?
GIVEN `the provision makes the act an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a provision of a written law make the act an offence?
      `the law shows a different intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law show an intention that an attempt should not be an offence?
GIVETH A STRING
`Can I be charged with an attempt to commit this offence` `the provision makes the act an offence` `the law shows a different intention` MEANS
    `the answer to a yes or no question` (`an attempt to commit the offence is an offence against the provision, punishable as if the offence itself had been committed` `the provision makes the act an offence` `the law shows a different intention`)

@export Does being penalised free me from paying damages to the person I injured (s 39)?
GIVEN `a penalty was imposed under a written law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was a penalty imposed under a written law?
      `the law expressly says otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law expressly provide that the penalty frees you from liability for damages?
GIVETH A STRING
`Does being penalised free me from paying damages to the person I injured` `a penalty was imposed under a written law` `the law expressly says otherwise` MEANS
    `the answer to a yes or no question` (`the penalty relieves the person from liability to answer for damages to a person injured` `a penalty was imposed under a written law` `the law expressly says otherwise`)

@export My act is an offence under two or more laws. Can I be punished twice for it (s 40)?
GIVEN `the number of laws that make my act or omission an offence` IS A NUMBER @desc Under how many written laws is your act or omission an offence?
      `the laws show a different intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the laws show an intention that you may be punished under more than one?
GIVETH A STRING
`My act is an offence under two or more laws can I be punished twice for it` `the number of laws that make my act or omission an offence` `the laws show a different intention` MEANS
    `the answer to a yes or no question` (`the offender is liable to be punished twice for the same offence` `the number of laws that make my act or omission an offence` `the laws show a different intention`)

@export What is the most I can be penalised under this law (s 41)?
GIVEN `the penalty the law provides` IS A NUMBER @desc The penalty the law provides for the offence, for example the fine in dollars
      `the law expressly says otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law expressly say that the penalty is not a maximum?
GIVETH A STRING
`What is the most I can be penalised under this law` `the penalty the law provides` `the law expressly says otherwise` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the most the offence is punishable by on conviction` `the penalty the law provides` `the law expressly says otherwise`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "The Interpretation Act does not say."
    WHEN JUST n THEN CONCAT "No more than ", TOSTRING n, "."

§ `Applications to Court (sections 41A to 41E)`

-- s 41A(1). Since 1 April 2022 a Court application under a law that prescribes no process (or an old one) is an originating application or a summons.
@export By what process must I make my application to Court (s 41A)?
GIVEN `the law provides for an application in civil proceedings to be made to a Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law provide for an application in civil proceedings to be made to a Court?
      `the day I make the application` IS A DATE @desc The day you make the application (YYYY-MM-DD)
      `the law prescribes no process` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law prescribe no process for making the application?
      `the law prescribes an old process` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law prescribe a petition, a motion, an originating motion or a summons in chambers?
      `my application starts the proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your application start the proceedings?
      `my application is made in proceedings already pending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your application made in proceedings that are already pending?
      `it is a petition of appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a petition of appeal?
      `it is in a class the Minister has excluded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it in a class of applications the Minister has excluded by order in the Gazette?
GIVETH A STRING
`By what process must I make my application to Court` `the law provides for an application in civil proceedings to be made to a Court` `the day I make the application` `the law prescribes no process` `the law prescribes an old process` `my application starts the proceedings` `my application is made in proceedings already pending` `it is a petition of appeal` `it is in a class the Minister has excluded` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the process the application must be made by` `the law provides for an application in civil proceedings to be made to a Court` `the day I make the application` `the law prescribes no process` `the law prescribes an old process` `my application starts the proceedings` `my application is made in proceedings already pending` `it is a petition of appeal` `it is in a class the Minister has excluded`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "The Interpretation Act does not say."
    WHEN JUST p THEN
        CONSIDER p
        WHEN `an originating application` THEN "By originating application."
        WHEN `a summons` THEN "By summons."

-- s 41D, 41E. An old word in a law (writ of summons, plaintiff ...) is read as its new word from 1 April 2022.
@export What does an old court term in a law now mean (ss 41D and 41E)?
GIVEN `the old or new expression` IS A STRING @desc The expression as the law words it, for example Writ of summons, Plaintiff, Leave of court
      `the day I read it` IS A DATE @desc The day the reference is being read (YYYY-MM-DD)
      `the proceedings` IS A STRING @desc The proceedings the reference is made in relation to. One of: Singapore International Commercial Court, Family Division, Family Court or Youth Court and commenced before 15 October 2024, any other
GIVETH A STRING
`What does an old court term in a law now mean` `the old or new expression` `the day I read it` `the proceedings` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the reference is construed as a reference to` `the old or new expression` `the day I read it` (`the proceedings named` `the proceedings`)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "The Interpretation Act does not say."
    WHEN JUST s THEN CONCAT "It is read as: ", s

§ `Fees (sections 45 and 46)`

-- s 45(1). An office may decline to act until the fee, or an estimate where the amount cannot be known, is paid.
@export Can the office refuse to do what I asked until I pay the fee (s 45(1))?
GIVEN `the office is required to do something for which a fee or charge is payable under a written law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person or office required to do something for which a fee is to be paid or a charge made under a written law?
      `the precise amount cannot be known until the thing is done` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can the exact amount not be known until the thing has been done?
      `I have paid the fee or charge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you paid the fee or charge?
      `I have paid the amount the office estimated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you paid the amount the office estimated to be correct?
GIVETH A STRING
`Can the office refuse to do what I asked until I pay the fee` `the office is required to do something for which a fee or charge is payable under a written law` `the precise amount cannot be known until the thing is done` `I have paid the fee or charge` `I have paid the amount the office estimated` MEANS
    `the answer to a yes or no question` (`the person, officer, department or authority may decline to do the thing` `the office is required to do something for which a fee or charge is payable under a written law` `the precise amount cannot be known until the thing is done` `I have paid the fee or charge` `I have paid the amount the office estimated`)

@export After an estimated fee, how much more must I pay, or how much will be refunded (s 45(2))?
GIVEN `the estimate I paid` IS A NUMBER @desc The estimated amount you paid
      `the correct amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The correct amount once the thing has been done
GIVETH A NUMBER
`After an estimated fee how much more must I pay or how much will be refunded` `the estimate I paid` `the correct amount` MEANS
    `the adjustment after the thing has been done` `the estimate I paid` `the correct amount`

§ `The Gazette (section 48)`

GIVEN x IS A STRING
GIVETH A MAYBE `Form of the Gazette`
`the form of the Gazette named` x MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF x EQUALS "the prescribed website" THEN JUST `the prescribed website`
        IF x EQUALS "print" THEN JUST `print`
        IF x EQUALS "another electronic form" THEN JUST `another electronic form`
        IF x EQUALS "another prescribed form" THEN JUST `another prescribed form`
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- s 48(6), (7). On or after the appointed date the Gazette on the prescribed website prevails over any other form; before it, print does.
-- "Yes." means the Act says this form prevails; "No." means the Act says the other form prevails over it; anything else is not covered.
@export If two forms of the Gazette differ, does this form control (s 48(6) and (7))?
GIVEN `the form I am asking about` IS A STRING @desc One of: the prescribed website, print, another electronic form, another prescribed form
      `the other form` IS A STRING @desc The form it differs from. One of: the prescribed website, print, another electronic form, another prescribed form
      `the day the Gazette was published` IS A DATE @desc The day the Gazette was published (YYYY-MM-DD)
      `the appointed date` IS A DATE @desc The date section 2 of the Electronic Gazette and Legislation Act 2025 commences (YYYY-MM-DD)
GIVETH A STRING
`If two forms of the Gazette differ does this form control` `the form I am asking about` `the other form` `the day the Gazette was published` `the appointed date` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the form of the Gazette named` `the form I am asking about`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "Please choose one of: the prescribed website, print, another electronic form, another prescribed form."
    WHEN JUST f THEN
        CONSIDER `the form of the Gazette named` `the other form`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN "Please choose one of: the prescribed website, print, another electronic form, another prescribed form."
        WHEN JUST g THEN `the answer to a yes or no question` (`this form prevails over the other form to the extent of any inconsistency` f g `the day the Gazette was published` `the appointed date`)

§ `Documents served on me, or by me (section 48A)`

GIVEN whom IS A STRING
GIVETH A MAYBE `Whom a document is served on`
`the recipient named` whom MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF whom EQUALS "an individual" THEN JUST `an individual`
        IF whom EQUALS "a partnership" THEN JUST `a partnership`
        IF whom EQUALS "a body corporate" THEN JUST `a body corporate`
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

GIVEN how IS A STRING
GIVETH A `How a document is served`
`the way of serving named` how MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF how EQUALS "delivering it to the individual personally" THEN `by delivering it to the individual personally`
        IF how EQUALS "leaving it at the individual's usual or last known address" THEN `by leaving it at the usual or last known address of the place of residence or business of the individual`
        IF how EQUALS "sending it by prepaid post to the individual's usual or last known address" THEN `by sending it by prepaid post to the usual or last known address of the place of residence or business of the individual`
        IF how EQUALS "delivering it to the secretary or like officer of the partnership" THEN `by delivering it to the secretary or other like officer of the partnership`
        IF how EQUALS "leaving it at the partnership's principal or last known place of business in Singapore" THEN `by leaving it at the principal or last known place of business of the partnership in Singapore`
        IF how EQUALS "sending it by prepaid post to the partnership's principal or last known place of business in Singapore" THEN `by sending it by prepaid post to the principal or last known place of business of the partnership in Singapore`
        IF how EQUALS "delivering it to the secretary or like officer of the body corporate" THEN `by delivering it to the secretary or other like officer of the body corporate`
        IF how EQUALS "leaving it at the body corporate's registered office or a principal office in Singapore" THEN `by leaving it at the registered office or a principal office of the body corporate in Singapore`
        IF how EQUALS "sending it by prepaid post to the body corporate's registered office or a principal office in Singapore" THEN `by sending it by prepaid post to the registered office or a principal office of the body corporate in Singapore`
        OTHERWISE `in some other way`

@export May a document be served on this person or body in this way (s 48A(1))?
GIVEN `the law authorises or requires the document to be served` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law authorise or require the document to be served on a person?
      `who it is served on` IS A STRING @desc One of: an individual, a partnership, a body corporate
      `how it is served` IS A STRING @desc For example: delivering it to the individual personally; leaving it at the individual's usual or last known address; sending it by prepaid post to the individual's usual or last known address; delivering it to the secretary or like officer of the partnership; leaving it at the partnership's principal or last known place of business in Singapore; sending it by prepaid post to the partnership's principal or last known place of business in Singapore; delivering it to the secretary or like officer of the body corporate; leaving it at the body corporate's registered office or a principal office in Singapore; sending it by prepaid post to the body corporate's registered office or a principal office in Singapore; or write anything else
      `the law shows a different intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law show an intention that the document should not be served this way?
GIVETH A STRING
`May a document be served on this person or body in this way` `the law authorises or requires the document to be served` `who it is served on` `how it is served` `the law shows a different intention` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the recipient named` `who it is served on`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "Please choose: an individual, a partnership or a body corporate."
    WHEN JUST w THEN `the answer to a yes or no question` (`the document may be served this way` `the law authorises or requires the document to be served` w (`the way of serving named` `how it is served`) `the law shows a different intention`)

§ `My time limit (sections 50 to 53)`

-- s 50(a), (b), (d). The day of the event is not counted. If the last day is a Sunday or a public holiday the period runs to the next day that is not.
-- A period of weeks, months or years, or one counted "after" an event, has no counting rule in the Act: the answer says so.
@export On which day does my period end (s 50)?
GIVEN `the day of the event or act` IS A DATE @desc The day the event happened or the act was done that the period runs from (YYYY-MM-DD)
      `how many` IS A NUMBER @desc How long is the period? For example 30 for 30 days, or 3 for 3 years.
      `the unit` IS A STRING @desc The unit the period is given in. One of: days, weeks, months, years
      `the law says from the event` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law count the period "from" the event or act? Answer no if it counts "after" it.
      `public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc The dates of public holidays in Singapore falling in or just after your period (YYYY-MM-DD each); leave empty if none
      `the law shows a different intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law that gives you the time show an intention that these counting rules should not apply?
GIVETH A STRING
`On which day does my period end` `the day of the event or act` `how many` `the unit` `the law says from the event` `public holidays` `the law shows a different intention` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the period unit named` `the unit`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "Please choose one of: days, weeks, months, years."
    WHEN JUST u THEN
        `the answer with a date` "Your period ends on "
            (`the last day of a period` `the day of the event or act` `how many` u
                (IF `the law says from the event` THEN From ELSE After) `public holidays` `the law shows a different intention`)

-- The Act has no rule for counting a period of weeks, months or years: the person who has worked out the last day asks what s 50(b) then does.
@export My period is in weeks, months or years and I have worked out its last day. Does s 50(b) move it (s 50(b))?
GIVEN `the last day as I worked it out` IS A DATE @desc The last day of your period as you worked it out yourself (YYYY-MM-DD). The Interpretation Act does not give a rule for counting weeks, months or years.
      `the unit` IS A STRING @desc The unit the period is given in. One of: days, weeks, months, years
      `public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc The dates of public holidays in Singapore falling in or just after your period (YYYY-MM-DD each); leave empty if none
      `the law shows a different intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law that gives you the time show an intention that these rules should not apply?
GIVETH A STRING
`My period is in weeks months or years and I have worked out its last day does section 50(b) move it` `the last day as I worked it out` `the unit` `public holidays` `the law shows a different intention` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the period unit named` `the unit`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "Please choose one of: days, weeks, months, years."
    WHEN JUST u THEN `the answer with a date` "Your period ends on " (`the last day of the period once section 50(b) is applied` `the last day as I worked it out` u `public holidays` `the law shows a different intention`)

@export Is the last day of my period one of the days the Act leaves out because it is a Sunday or a public holiday (s 50(b))?
GIVEN `the last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day of your period (YYYY-MM-DD)
      `public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc The dates of public holidays in Singapore near that day (YYYY-MM-DD each); leave empty if none
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Is the last day of my period an excluded day` `the last day` `public holidays` MEANS
    `is an excluded day` `the last day` `public holidays`

-- s 50(c). A thing to be done on a certain day, if that is a Sunday or public holiday, is in time if done on the next day that is not.
@export The day I was told to act on is a Sunday or a public holiday. Am I in time if I act the next working day (s 50(c))?
GIVEN `the day I was directed or allowed to act on` IS A DATE @desc The day the thing is directed or allowed to be done on (YYYY-MM-DD)
      `the day I did it` IS A DATE @desc The day you did it (YYYY-MM-DD)
      `public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc The dates of public holidays in Singapore near those days (YYYY-MM-DD each); leave empty if none
      `the law shows a different intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law show an intention that this rule should not apply?
GIVETH A STRING
`The day I was told to act on is a Sunday or a public holiday am I in time if I act the next working day` `the day I was directed or allowed to act on` `the day I did it` `public holidays` `the law shows a different intention` MEANS
    CONSIDER `section 50(c) treats the act as done in due time` `the day I was directed or allowed to act on` `the day I did it` `public holidays` `the law shows a different intention`
    WHEN JUST b THEN "Yes: the Act treats it as done in due time."
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "Section 50(c) does not decide this."

@export What is the last day I may act on, if the day I was told to act on is a Sunday or a public holiday (s 50(c))?
GIVEN `the day I was directed or allowed to act on` IS A DATE @desc The day the thing is directed or allowed to be done on (YYYY-MM-DD)
      `public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc The dates of public holidays in Singapore near that day (YYYY-MM-DD each); leave empty if none
      `the law shows a different intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law show an intention that this rule should not apply?
GIVETH A STRING
`What is the last day I may act on` `the day I was directed or allowed to act on` `public holidays` `the law shows a different intention` MEANS
    `the answer with a date` "You may act up to and including " (`the last day for an act directed for a day` `the day I was directed or allowed to act on` `public holidays` `the law shows a different intention`)

-- s 50(d). In a time not exceeding 6 days, Sundays and public holidays are not reckoned.
@export Do Sundays and public holidays count in my period (s 50(d))?
GIVEN `the number of days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days does the law give you?
GIVETH A STRING
`Do Sundays and public holidays count in my period` `the number of days` MEANS
    IF `excluded days are not reckoned` `the number of days`
    THEN "No: in a time not exceeding 6 days, Sundays and public holidays are not counted."
    ELSE "The Interpretation Act does not leave them out of a period of this length when counting its days, though s 50(b) moves a last day that falls on one."

-- s 51. The clock the Act uses.
@export What clock does the law use, in hours ahead of UTC (s 51)?
GIVEN `the President has notified a different standard time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the President notified, in the Gazette, a standard time other than 8 hours ahead of UTC?
      `the notified hours ahead of UTC` IS A NUMBER @desc If so, how many hours ahead of UTC? (Enter 8 if none was notified.)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What clock does the law use` `the President has notified a different standard time` `the notified hours ahead of UTC` MEANS
    `standard time, in hours ahead of UTC` (IF `the President has notified a different standard time` THEN JUST `the notified hours ahead of UTC` ELSE NOTHING)

@export Is a time of day in the law standard time (s 51(1))?
GIVEN `the law or instrument specifically states another time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law or instrument specifically state that the time is not standard time?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Is a time of day in the law standard time` `the law or instrument specifically states another time` MEANS
    `a time of day is held to be standard time` `the law or instrument specifically states another time`

-- s 52.
@export How soon must I do a thing for which the law sets no time (s 52)?
GIVEN `the law prescribes or allows a time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law prescribe or allow a time within which the thing is to be done?
GIVETH A STRING
`How soon must I do a thing for which the law sets no time` `the law prescribes or allows a time` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the time for doing a thing` `the law prescribes or allows a time`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "Section 52 does not apply: the law sets a time."
    WHEN JUST t THEN "With all convenient speed, and as often as the occasion arises."

-- s 53.
@export My time has run out. May the court or authority still extend it if I apply late (s 53)?
GIVEN `the law gives a court or authority power to extend the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law give a court or other authority power to extend the time?
      `the law shows a different intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law show an intention that an application after the time has run out may not be considered?
GIVETH A STRING
`My time has run out may the court or authority still extend it if I apply late` `the law gives a court or authority power to extend the time` `the law shows a different intention` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the power to extend may be exercised after the time has run out` `the law gives a court or authority power to extend the time` `the law shows a different intention`
    WHEN JUST b THEN "Yes: the power may be exercised although the application is made after the time has run out."
    WHEN NOTHING THEN "The Interpretation Act does not say."
